    kind: work_item  # 345c
    title: "CLI argparse silently accepts top-level --backend flag placed after subcommand"  # 345c
    status: todo_hard  # 345c
    priority: 3  # 345c
    parent:  # 345c
    tags:  # 345c
    - developer_experience  # 345c
    isbefore: []  # 345c
    duplicate_of: []  # 345c
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 345c
    pr_ref:  # 345c
    description: "## Symptom\n\n`hypergumbo run --backend rust-analyzer <target>` is silently accepted by argparse without engaging the backend. The `--backend` flag is registered at the **top level** (cli.py:4860, choices=['tree-sitter', 'rust-analyzer']) and must appear **before** the subcommand. When placed after, argparse produces no error, but `should_use_rust_analyzer_backend` never sees the flag and the SCIP backend code path is never entered.\n\nThe expected behavior is one of:\n- argparse rejects the misplaced flag with 'unrecognized arguments: --backend rust-analyzer', OR\n- `--backend` is also registered on the `run` subparser (and other subparsers) so both placements work.\n\n## Discovery context\n\nSurfaced by hg-uat-v4.1.0 Round 09 (now deprecated) and explicitly confirmed by Round 10's plan (which moves the flag to the correct top-level position). Round 09's command was `hypergumbo run … --backend rust-analyzer …`; the artifact's `analysis_runs` metadata contained 136 entries identical to Round 04, with **zero** scip / rust-analyzer pass entries. Stderrs were byte-identical. The SCIP indexer never ran, but Round 09 still produced a complete artifact and an apparent 'Outcome (A) — bit-identical' verdict that was actually 'the same default-backend code path ran in both rounds'.\n\n## Impact\n\nThe class of bugs this enables: any agent or human who runs `hypergumbo <subcommand> --backend <choice>` (the natural-feeling form) gets silent fall-through to the default backend with no warning. Investigation rounds that depend on backend differences (like Round 09) can produce false-confirming verdicts. The flag-positioning rule isn't currently called out in `hypergumbo --help` either.\n\n## Fix scope (two options)\n\n(a) Tighten argparse: ensure subparsers reject unknown trailing flags. Likely a small parser config change.\n(b) Plumb `--backend` down to each subparser so it works in both positions. More invasive but more user-friendly.\n\nBoth fixes are 1-2 hour changes. Recommend (a) for correctness — explicit error is the right response to a misplaced flag; users learn the rule once and move on. Optional (b) on top is purely UX polish.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `hypergumbo run --backend rust-analyzer <target>` exits non-zero with an 'unrecognized arguments' error, OR\n- The flag is plumbed down and the run actually engages the SCIP backend (verifiable by `scip` or `rust_analyzer` pass appearing in the result's `analysis_runs`).\n- Property test: a regression case that asserts the chosen behavior, so the silent-accept never returns.\n\n## References\n\n- Round 09 (deprecated): `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/bakeoff_artifacts/hg-uat-v4.1.0/lab-notebook/deprecated_rounds/round-09-rust-analyzer-backend-replication/`\n- Round 10 (corrected, planned): `~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/bakeoff_artifacts/hg-uat-v4.1.0/lab-notebook/round-10-rust-analyzer-backend-replication/`\n- gate.py opt-in: `packages/hypergumbo-lang-rust-analyzer/src/hypergumbo_lang_rust_analyzer/gate.py:57-91`\n- top-level flag definition: `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/cli.py:4860-4861`"  # 345c
    fields: {}  # 345c
- op: update  # bdc9
  at: '2026-05-08T23:34:59Z'  # bdc9
